@subsection Streams Are Delayed Lists

As we saw in @ref{2.2.3}, sequences can serve as standard interfaces
for combining program modules.  We formulated powerful abstractions for
manipulating sequences, such as @code{mapSeq}, @code{filterSeq}, and
@code{accumulateSeq}, that capture a wide variety of operations in a manner that
is both succinct and elegant.

Unfortunately, if we represent sequences as lists, this elegance is bought at
the price of severe inefficiency with respect to both the time and space
required by our computations.  When we represent manipulations on sequences as
transformations of lists, our programs must construct and copy data structures
(which may be huge) at every step of a process.

To see why this is true, let us compare two programs for computing the sum of
all the prime numbers in an interval.  The first program is written in standard
iterative style:@footnote{Assume that we have a predicate @code{isPrime} (e.g.,
as in @ref{1.2.6}) that tests for primality.}

@example kotlin
fun sumPrimesByLoop(a: Long, b: Long): Long @{
    var total = 0L
    for (n in a..b) @{
        if (isPrime(n)) @{
            total += n
        @}
    @}
    return total
@}
@end example

@noindent
The second program performs the same computation in the signal-flow style of
@ref{2.2.3}, over the stream operations this section defines:

@example kotlin
fun sumPrimes(a: Long, b: Long): Long =
    streamFold(streamFilter(::isPrime, streamEnumerateInterval(a, b)), 0L, Long::plus)

sumPrimes(10000L, 1000000L)
// => 37544665627
@end example

@noindent
In carrying out the computation, the first program needs to store only the sum
being accumulated.  The same signal flow written over the eager list operations
of @ref{2.2.3} cannot do any testing until @code{enumerateInterval} has
constructed a complete list of the numbers in the interval; the filter
generates another complete list, which the folding step then collapses to form
a sum.  Such large intermediate storage is the price of the list
representation, and it is what the stream form above refuses to pay: each stage
runs only when the next stage asks it for values, so no stage is ever built in
full.  The inefficiency in using lists becomes painfully apparent if we use the
sequence paradigm to compute the second prime in the interval from 10,000 to
1,000,000 by evaluating the expression

@example kotlin
filterSeq(::isPrime, enumerateInterval(10000L, 1000000L))[1]
@end example

@noindent
This expression does find the second prime, but the computational overhead is
outrageous.  We construct a list of almost a million integers, filter this list
by testing each element for primality, and then ignore almost all of the
result.  In a more traditional programming style, we would interleave the
enumeration and the filtering, and stop when we reached the second prime.

Streams are a clever idea that allows one to use sequence manipulations without
incurring the costs of manipulating sequences as lists.  With streams we can
achieve the best of both worlds: We can formulate programs elegantly as
sequence manipulations, while attaining the efficiency of incremental
computation.  The basic idea is to arrange to construct a stream only
partially, and to pass the partial construction to the program that consumes
the stream.  If the consumer attempts to access a part of the stream that has
not yet been constructed, the stream will automatically construct just enough
more of itself to produce the required part, thus preserving the illusion that
the entire stream exists.  In other words, although we will write programs as
if we were processing complete sequences, we design our stream implementation
to automatically and transparently interleave the construction of the stream
with its use.

On the surface, streams are just lists with different names for the functions
that manipulate them.  There is a constructor, @code{consStream}, and two
selectors, @code{streamHead} and @code{streamTail}, which satisfy the
constraints

@example kotlin
consStream(x) @{ y @}.streamHead() == x
consStream(x) @{ y @}.streamTail() == y
@end example

@noindent
There is a distinguishable object, @code{LStream.Empty}, which cannot be the
result of any @code{consStream} operation, and which the branches of the sealed
@code{LStream} interface identify.@footnote{In this edition the empty stream is
the @code{Empty} object of the sealed interface @code{LStream}, so the book's
@code{stream-null?} is the @code{when} branch that matches
@code{LStream.Empty}.}  Thus we can make and use streams, in just the same way
as we can make and use lists, to represent aggregate data arranged in a
sequence.  In particular, we can build stream analogs of the list operations
from @ref{Chapter 2}, such as @code{streamRef}, @code{streamMap}, and
@code{streamForEach}:@footnote{This should bother you.  The fact that we are
defining such similar functions for streams and lists indicates that we are
missing some underlying abstraction.  Unfortunately, in order to exploit this
abstraction, we will need to exert finer control over the process of evaluation
than we can at present.  We will discuss this point further at the end of
@ref{3.5.4}.  In @ref{4.2}, we'll develop a framework that unifies lists and
streams.}

@example kotlin
public fun <T> streamRef(s: LStream<T>, n: Int): T @{
    var cursor = s
    repeat(n) @{ cursor = cursor.streamTail() @}
    return checkNotNull(cursor.streamHead()) @{ "stream-ref $n past the end of the stream" @}
@}

public fun <T, R> streamMap(f: (T) -> R, s: LStream<T>): LStream<R> =
    when (s) @{
        is LStream.Empty -> LStream.Empty
        is LStream.Cons -> consStream(f(s.head)) @{ streamMap(f, s.tail) @}
    @}

public fun <T> streamForEach(action: (T) -> Unit, s: LStream<T>) @{
    var cursor = s
    while (cursor is LStream.Cons) @{
        action(cursor.head)
        cursor = cursor.tail
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{streamForEach} is useful for viewing streams; in this edition the
rendering comes back as one string, one line per element, so the output is a
value a test can hold:

@example kotlin
public fun displayStream(s: LStream<*>): String = buildString @{
    var cursor: LStream<*> = s
    while (cursor is LStream.Cons) @{
        append(cursor.head.toString()).append('\n')
        cursor = cursor.tail
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Kotlin ships its own lazy sequence type: the stdlib @code{Sequence}.  A
@code{Sequence} pipeline runs on demand, so it interleaves stages the way the
stream idea promises, and a one-shot query like the second prime above never
materializes the interval.  It is also cold and unmemoized: nothing in a
@code{Sequence} remembers that a value was computed, so a second walk over the
same pipeline recomputes every element from the source, and a self-referential
definition like @code{fibs}, whose every element is defined in terms of earlier
ones, cannot be written on it at all, because a local value cannot appear in
its own initializer and a @code{Sequence} has no cell in which a computed tail
could be stored.  The exercises of this section measure exactly this
difference, so the edition switches representation here: everything where
@code{consStream} semantics are load-bearing runs on the memoized
@code{LStream} type introduced below, whose tails are @code{by lazy} values,
forced once and shared by every later reader.  The finite pipeline sketches
that need no sharing, the sieve sketch of @ref{3.5.2} and the maps of exercise
@ref{Exercise 3.50}, stay on the cold @code{Sequence}.

@noindent
To make the stream implementation automatically and transparently interleave
the construction of a stream with its use, we will arrange for the tail of a
stream node to be computed when it is first read by @code{streamTail} rather
than when the node is constructed by @code{consStream}.  This implementation
choice is reminiscent of our discussion of rational numbers in @ref{2.1.2},
where we saw that we can choose to implement rational numbers so that the
reduction of numerator and denominator to lowest terms is performed either at
construction time or at selection time.  The two rational-number
implementations produce the same data abstraction, but the choice has an effect
on efficiency.  There is a similar relationship between streams and ordinary
lists.  As a data abstraction, streams are the same as lists.  The difference
is the time at which the elements are evaluated.  With ordinary lists, an
element and its rest are computed when the list is built.  With an
@code{LStream} node, the head is computed at construction time and the tail
only when @code{streamTail} first reads it.

Our implementation of streams is based on Kotlin's @code{lazy} value.
Evaluating @code{lazy @{ ⟨@var{exp}⟩ @}} does not evaluate the expression
@code{⟨@var{exp}⟩}, but rather returns a so-called @newterm{delayed object},
which we can think of as a ``promise'' to evaluate @code{⟨@var{exp}⟩} at some
future time.  As a companion, reading the object's @code{.value} performs the
evaluation---in effect, forcing the @code{lazy} to fulfill its promise.  We
will see below how the package works, but first let us use it to construct
streams.

@code{consStream} is defined so that

@example kotlin
consStream(⟨@var{a}⟩) @{ ⟨@var{b}⟩ @}
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example kotlin
LStream.Cons(⟨@var{a}⟩) @{ ⟨@var{b}⟩ @}
@end example

@noindent
What this means is that we will construct streams out of nodes of the runtime
type @code{LStream}:

@example kotlin
public sealed interface LStream<out T> @{
    public data object Empty : LStream<Nothing>

    public class Cons<T>(
        public val head: T,
        tailThunk: () -> LStream<T>,
    ) : LStream<T> @{
        public val tail: LStream<T> by lazy(LazyThreadSafetyMode.NONE, tailThunk)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The head is computed at once, at cons time.  The tail thunk is wrapped in a
@code{by lazy} value: rather than placing the value of the rest of the stream
into the node, we put there a promise to compute the rest if it is ever
requested, and the promise is kept at most once, so every later reader shares
the computed node.  @code{streamHead} and @code{streamTail} can now be defined
as functions:

@example kotlin
public fun <T> LStream<T>.streamHead(): T? = (this as? LStream.Cons)?.head

public fun <T> LStream<T>.streamTail(): LStream<T> =
    when (this) @{
        is LStream.Empty -> LStream.Empty
        is LStream.Cons -> tail
    @}
@end example

@noindent
@code{streamHead} selects the head of the node; @code{streamTail} forces the
memoized tail and returns the rest of the stream.@footnote{Although
@code{streamHead} and @code{streamTail} are ordinary functions,
@code{consStream} takes the tail as a lambda.  If @code{consStream} took the
tail stream as a plain argument, Kotlin would evaluate
@code{⟨@var{b}⟩} before the call, which is precisely what we do not want to
happen.  For the same reason @code{lazy} takes its body as a lambda rather than
as a value, though reading @code{.value} is an ordinary property read.}

@subsubheading The stream implementation in action

To see how this implementation behaves, let us analyze the ``outrageous'' prime
computation we saw above, reformulated in terms of streams:

@example kotlin
streamFilter(::isPrime, streamEnumerateInterval(10000L, 1000000L))
    .streamTail()
    .streamHead()
// => 10009
@end example

@noindent
We will see that it does indeed work efficiently.

We begin by calling @code{streamEnumerateInterval} with the arguments 10,000
and 1,000,000.  @code{streamEnumerateInterval} is the stream analog of
@code{enumerateInterval} (@ref{2.2.3}):

@example kotlin
public fun streamEnumerateInterval(low: Long, high: Long): LStream<Long> =
    if (low > high) LStream.Empty else consStream(low) @{ streamEnumerateInterval(low + 1L, high) @}
@end example

@noindent
and thus the result returned by @code{streamEnumerateInterval}, formed by the
@code{consStream}, is@footnote{The numbers shown here do not really appear in
the delayed expression.  What actually appears is the original expression, in
an environment in which the variables are bound to the appropriate numbers.
For example, @code{low + 1L} with @code{low} bound to 10,000 actually appears
where @code{10001L} is shown.}

@example kotlin
consStream(10000L) @{ streamEnumerateInterval(10001L, 1000000L) @}
@end example

@noindent
That is, @code{streamEnumerateInterval} returns a stream whose head is 10,000
and whose tail is a promise to enumerate more of the interval if so requested.
This stream is now filtered for primes, using the stream analog of the
@code{filter} procedure (@ref{2.2.3}):

@example kotlin
public fun <T> streamFilter(p: (T) -> Boolean, s: LStream<T>): LStream<T> =
    when (s) @{
        is LStream.Empty -> LStream.Empty
        is LStream.Cons ->
            if (p(s.head)) @{
                consStream(s.head) @{ streamFilter(p, s.tail) @}
            @} else @{
                streamFilter(p, s.tail)
            @}
    @}
@end example

@noindent
@code{streamFilter} tests the head of the stream (the head of the node, which
is 10,000).  Since this is not prime, @code{streamFilter} examines the tail of
its input stream.  The call to @code{streamTail} forces the delayed
@code{streamEnumerateInterval}, which now returns

@example kotlin
consStream(10001L) @{ streamEnumerateInterval(10002L, 1000000L) @}
@end example

@noindent
@code{streamFilter} now looks at the head of this stream, 10,001, sees that
this is not prime either, forces another @code{streamTail}, and so on, until
@code{streamEnumerateInterval} yields the prime 10,007, whereupon
@code{streamFilter}, according to its definition, returns

@example kotlin
consStream(s.head) @{ streamFilter(p, s.tail) @}
@end example

@noindent
which in this case is

@example kotlin
consStream(10007L) @{
    streamFilter(
        ::isPrime,
        consStream(10008L) @{ streamEnumerateInterval(10009L, 1000000L) @},
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
This result is now passed to @code{streamTail} in our original expression.
This forces the delayed @code{streamFilter}, which in turn keeps forcing the
delayed @code{streamEnumerateInterval} until it finds the next prime, which
is 10,009.  Finally, the result passed to @code{streamHead} in our original
expression is

@example kotlin
consStream(10009L) @{
    streamFilter(
        ::isPrime,
        consStream(10010L) @{ streamEnumerateInterval(10011L, 1000000L) @},
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{streamHead} returns 10,009, and the computation is complete.  Only as
many integers were tested for primality as were necessary to find the second
prime, and the interval was enumerated only as far as was necessary to feed the
prime filter.

In general, we can think of delayed evaluation as ``demand-driven''
programming, whereby each stage in the stream process is activated only enough
to satisfy the next stage.  What we have done is to decouple the actual order
of events in the computation from the apparent structure of our procedures.  We
write programs as if the streams existed ``all at once'' when, in reality,
the computation is performed incrementally, as in traditional programming
styles.

@subsubheading Implementing @code{delay} and @code{force}

Although @code{lazy} and @code{.value} may seem like mysterious operations,
their implementation is really quite straightforward.  A delay must package an
expression so that it can be evaluated later on demand, and Kotlin accomplishes
this simply by treating the expression as the body of a lambda of no arguments.
The package is

@example kotlin
lazy @{ ⟨@var{exp}⟩ @}
@end example

@noindent
and the raw material it wraps is the lambda

@example kotlin
@{ ⟨@var{exp}⟩ @}
@end example

@noindent
Force simply reads what the package holds, so the language already implements
it: forcing the delayed object is reading its @code{.value} property,

@example kotlin
delayed.value
@end example

@noindent
This implementation suffices for @code{lazy} and @code{.value} to work as
advertised, but there is an important optimization included as well.  In many
applications, we end up forcing the same delayed object many times.  This can
lead to serious inefficiency in recursive programs involving streams.  (See
@ref{Exercise 3.57}.)  The solution is to build delayed objects so that the
first time they are forced, they store the value that is computed.  Subsequent
forcings will simply return the stored value without repeating the computation.
In other words, the delay of this edition is a special-purpose memoized
procedure similar to the one described in @ref{Exercise 3.27}.  Kotlin's
@code{lazy} is exactly that: the first read of @code{.value} runs the lambda
and stores the result, and every later read returns the stored result.  One way
to build such a thing ourselves is a function that takes as argument a lambda
of no arguments and returns a memoized version of it.  The first time the
memoized lambda is run, it saves the computed result.  On subsequent
evaluations, it simply returns the result.

@example kotlin
public fun <T> memoProc(f: () -> T): () -> T @{
    var alreadyRun = false
    var result: T? = null
    return @{
        if (!alreadyRun) @{
            result = f()
            alreadyRun = true
        @}
        checkNotNull(result)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{lazy @{ ⟨@var{exp}⟩ @}} is then equivalent to

@example kotlin
memoProc @{ ⟨@var{exp}⟩ @}
@end example

@noindent
and @code{.value} is the force as defined previously.  The stream nodes above
declare their tails @code{by lazy}, so each tail thunk runs at most once no
matter how many readers force it.@footnote{There are many possible
implementations of streams other than the one described in this section.
Delayed evaluation, which is the key to making streams practical, was inherent
in Algol 60's @newterm{call-by-name} parameter-passing method.  The use of this
mechanism to implement streams was first described by @ref{Landin (1965)}.  Delayed
evaluation for streams was introduced into Lisp by @ref{Friedman and Wise (1976)}. In
their implementation, @code{cons} always delays evaluating its arguments, so
that lists automatically behave as streams.  The memoizing optimization is also
known as @newterm{call-by-need}.  The Algol community would refer to our
original delayed objects as @newterm{call-by-name thunks} and to the optimized
versions as @newterm{call-by-need thunks}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.50}Exercise 3.50:} Complete the following
definition, which generalizes @code{streamMap} to allow functions that take
multiple arguments, analogous to @code{map} in @ref{2.2.1},
@ref{Footnote 78}.  In this edition the argument streams arrive as a list of
cold @code{Sequence}s.

@example kotlin
fun <T, R> streamMapN(proc: (List<T>) -> R, streams: List<Sequence<T>>): Sequence<R> =
    if (streams.first().⟨??⟩)
        emptySequence()
    else
        sequenceOf(proc(streams.map(⟨??⟩))) ⟨??⟩
            streamMapN(proc, streams.map(⟨??⟩))
@end example

@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.51}Exercise 3.51:} In order to take a closer look at
delayed evaluation, we will use the following function, which simply returns
its argument after recording it in a log:

@example kotlin
public fun <T> show(x: T, log: MutableList<String>): T @{
    log.add(x.toString())
    return x
@}
@end example

What does the log hold in response to evaluating each expression in the
following sequence?@footnote{Exercises such as @ref{Exercise 3.51} and
@ref{Exercise 3.52} are valuable for testing our understanding of how
@code{lazy} works.  On the other hand, intermixing delayed evaluation with
logging---and, even worse, with assignment---is extremely confusing, and
instructors of courses on computer languages have traditionally tormented their
students with examination questions such as the ones in this section.  Needless
to say, writing programs that depend on such subtleties is odious programming
style.  Part of the power of stream processing is that it lets us ignore the
order in which events actually happen in our programs.  Unfortunately, this is
precisely what we cannot afford to do in the presence of assignment, which
forces us to be concerned with time and change.}

@example kotlin
val log = mutableListOf<String>()

val x = streamMap(@{ n -> show(n, log) @}, streamEnumerateInterval(0L, 10L))
// -- 0

streamRef(x, 5)
// => 5
// -- 1
// -- 2
// -- 3
// -- 4
// -- 5

streamRef(x, 7)
// => 7
// -- 6
// -- 7
@end example

The lines marked @code{--} land in the log, one per @code{show} call.  The
definition itself records @code{0}, because a mapped head is computed at cons
time; the first @code{streamRef} adds 1 through 5 as the memoized tails are
forced; the second adds 6 and 7 and nothing else.  With an unmemoized delay,
the second walk would re-record 1 through 5.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.52}Exercise 3.52:} Consider the sequence of
expressions

@example kotlin
var sum = 0L

fun accum(x: Long): Long @{
    sum += x
    return sum
@}

val seq = streamMap(::accum, streamEnumerateInterval(1L, 20L))

val y = streamFilter(@{ it % 2L == 0L @}, seq)

val z = streamFilter(@{ it % 5L == 0L @}, seq)

streamRef(y, 7)
displayStream(z)
@end example

What is the value of @code{sum} after each of the above expressions is
evaluated?  What is the string that the @code{displayStream} expression
returns?  Would these responses differ if every tail were packaged as the plain
lambda @code{@{ ⟨@var{exp}⟩ @}}, run again on each read, without the memoization
provided by @code{memoProc}?  Explain.
@end quotation
