@node	3.5.4, 3.5.5, 3.5.3, 3.5
@subsection Streams and Delayed Evaluation

The @code{integral} function at the end of the preceding section shows how we
can use streams to model signal-processing systems that contain feedback loops.
The feedback loop for the adder shown in @ref{Figure 3.32} is modeled by the
fact that @code{integral}'s internal stream @code{int} is defined in terms of
itself:

@example kotlin
var int: LStream<Double>? = null
val tied: LStream<Double> =
    consStream(initialValue) {
        addStreams(scaleStream(integrand, dt), checkNotNull(int) { "int not yet tied" })
    }
int = tied
@end example

@noindent
This shape is legal only because the tail lambda reads the cell after the last
line has tied it.  Kotlin's ability to deal with such an implicit definition
depends on the delay that is incorporated into @code{consStream}.  Without this
delay, the stream could not be constructed before evaluating both arguments to
@code{consStream}, which would require that @code{int} already be defined.  In
general, delay is crucial for using streams to model signal-processing systems
that contain loops.  Without delay, our models would have to be formulated so
that the inputs to any signal-processing component would be fully evaluated
before the output could be produced.  This would outlaw loops.

@noindent
Unfortunately, stream models of systems with loops may require uses of
delay beyond the ``hidden'' delay supplied by @code{consStream}.
For instance, @ref{Figure 3.34} shows a signal-processing system for solving
the differential equation @math{{dy / dt = f(y)}} where @math{f} is a given
function.  The figure shows a mapping component, which applies @math{f} to its
input signal, linked in a feedback loop to an integrator in a manner very
similar to that of the analog computer circuits that are actually used to solve
such equations.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.34}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.34:} An ``analog computer circuit'' that solves the equation @math{{dy / dt = f(y)}}.

@example
                            y_0
                             |
                             V
    +----------+  dy   +----------+     y
+-->|  map: f  +------>| integral +--*----->
|   +----------+       +----------+  |
|                                    |
+------------------------------------+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.34,,110mm,Figure 3.34: An ``analog computer circuit'' that solves the equation dy / dt = f(y).,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
