@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.34:} An ``analog computer circuit'' that solves the equation @math{{dy / dt = f(y)}}.}
@end float

Assuming we are given an initial value @math{y_0} for @math{y}, we could try to model
this system using the function

@example kotlin
fun solve(f: (Double) -> Double, y0: Double, dt: Double): LStream<Double> {
    val y: LStream<Double> = integral(dy, y0, dt)
    val dy: LStream<Double> = streamMap(f, y)
    return y
}
@end example
This procedure does not compile, because in the first line of @code{solve} the
call to @code{integral} requires that the input @code{dy} be defined, which
does not happen until the second line of @code{solve}.

On the other hand, the intent of our definition does make sense, because we
can, in principle, begin to generate the @code{y} stream without knowing
@code{dy}.  Indeed, @code{integral} and many other stream operations have
properties similar to those of @code{consStream}, in that we can generate part
of the answer given only partial information about the arguments.  For
@code{integral}, the first element of the output stream is the specified
@code{initialValue}.  Thus, we can generate the first element of the output
stream without evaluating the integrand @code{dy}.  Once we know the first
element of @code{y}, the @code{streamMap} in the second line of @code{solve}
can begin working to generate the first element of @code{dy}, which will
produce the next element of @code{y}, and so on.

To take advantage of this idea, we will redefine @code{integral} to expect the
integrand stream to be a @newterm{delayed argument}: a @code{Lazy} value.
@code{integral} reads the integrand's @code{.value} only when it is required
to generate more than the first element of the output stream:

@example kotlin
fun integral(
    delayedIntegrand: Lazy<LStream<Double>>,
    initialValue: Double,
    dt: Double,
): LStream<Double> {
    var int: LStream<Double>? = null
    val tied: LStream<Double> =
        consStream(initialValue) {
            addStreams(scaleStream(delayedIntegrand.value, dt), checkNotNull(int) { "int not yet tied" })
        }
    int = tied
    return tied
}
@end example

@noindent
The book's internal definition of @code{int} becomes Kotlin's assigned-once
cell: a local cannot mention itself in its own initializer, so @code{int} is a
nullable cell that the tail lambda reads, and the last line ties the cell
before anything can force a tail.  Now we can implement our @code{solve}
function, tying the integrand lazily in the definition of
@code{y}:@footnote{The book's version depends on the order of internal
definitions, and Scheme implementations differ on whether it works.  Kotlin's
lazy tie replaces that order dependence: the @code{lazy} value is not read
until @code{integral} forces it past the first element, so the definition
order of @code{y} and its integrand no longer matters.  Because a local may
not be read inside its own initializer line, the library form ties @code{y}
through the once-assigned cell shown in @code{integral} above.  (See
@ref{4.1.6}.)}

@example kotlin
fun solve(
    f: (Double) -> Double,
    y0: Double,
    dt: Double,
): LStream<Double> {
    var dy: LStream<Double>? = null
    val y: LStream<Double> = integral(lazy { checkNotNull(dy) { "dy not yet tied" } }, y0, dt)
    dy = streamMap(f, y)
    return y
}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{dy} cell is assigned exactly once, before any force can reach it: the
delayed integrand is the book's @code{(delay dy)}, tied back to @code{y}
itself inside the lazy value.

@noindent
In general, every caller of @code{integral} must now pass the integrand as a
@code{lazy { ... }} value.  We can demonstrate that the @code{solve} function
works by approximating @math{{e \approx 2.718}} by computing the value at
@math{{y = 1}} of the solution to the differential equation
@math{{dy / dt = y}} with initial condition @math{{y(0) = 1}}:

@example kotlin
streamRef(solve({ y -> y }, 1.0, 0.001), 1000)
// => 2.716923932235896 (the book's 2.716924 rounded)
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.77}Exercise 3.77:} The @code{integral} function
used above was analogous to the ``implicit'' definition of the infinite stream
of integers in @ref{3.5.2}.  Alternatively, we can give a definition of
@code{integral} that is more like @code{integersStartingFrom} (also in
@ref{3.5.2}):

@example kotlin
fun integral(
    integrand: LStream<Double>,
    initialValue: Double,
    dt: Double,
): LStream<Double> =
    consStream(initialValue) {
        val head = integrand.streamHead()
        if (head == null) {
            LStream.Empty
        } else {
            integral(integrand.streamTail(), dt * head + initialValue, dt)
        }
    }
@end example

When used in systems with loops, this function has the same problem as does
our original version of @code{integral}.  Modify the function so that it
expects the @code{integrand} as a delayed argument and hence can be used in
the @code{solve} function shown above.
@end quotation

